Unified effect read-lease design freeze:

- baseline: `EffectLog` currently moves either the FIFO head or latest reset
  through `queued/latest_generation_reset -> active -> queued/removed` and
  advances `ApplySequence` both at checkout and settlement. External I/O still
  occurs outside the guard, but checkout is a non-semantic authority write;
- ordinary-only borrowing was considered and rejected before implementation.
  It would add a read-borrow protocol while preserving a second active-checkout
  protocol solely for rare reset work, increasing transition and test surface;
- selected model: a committed effect remains in exactly one authoritative log
  location until settlement. The sole publisher borrows an immutable receipt
  for the minimum sequence of the charged FIFO head and coalesced reset. Local
  endpoint progress is tentative evidence. One settlement Plan validates and
  atomically commits the advanced cursor or terminal removal;
- queued envelopes and generation resets become distinct concrete resident
  types. A charged queued record cannot carry `class = None`, and a reset
  cannot acquire a charged class. The resident `active` variant and generic
  checkout mutation disappear;
- a queued head is stable while borrowed: every producer can only append a
  strictly higher sequence, replace the coalesced reset, or close production.
  None can remove, reorder or mutate that head. Settlement therefore validates
  exact source, sequence, Arc identity and original cursor before retaining or
  removing it;
- a borrowed reset may be replaced by a newer reset while I/O is in flight.
  Exact-current settlement updates/removes that reset. A strictly newer reset
  yields a typed mutation-free `Superseded` outcome and the old receipt retires
  outside the guard; it can never overwrite or resurrect the newer reset.
  Missing, older or identity-mismatched residents remain structural faults;
- `AuthorityEffectPublisherClaim` becomes the compile-time exclusivity proof,
  not an unused RAII flag. Acquisition requires its mutable borrow and returns
  a publication lease whose lifetime retains that borrow. Safe code therefore
  cannot acquire a second receipt or drop/release the claim while a receipt is
  live. The production lease remains bound to the runtime that issued it;
- cancellation retains the exact completed endpoint prefix through the same
  settlement path. For a superseded reset, the newer full reset subsumes that
  prefix. The existing unavoidable current-endpoint action/acknowledgement
  window remains unchanged;
- resource and pending-reject charge stays resident until terminal settlement.
  External endpoint I/O remains lock-free. Close stays level-triggered: an idle
  closed log wakes the publisher, while a borrowed resident keeps the log
  observably non-drained until settlement;
- expected mechanical change per ordinary batch is one authority read and one
  settlement write instead of checkout write plus settlement write. It also
  removes one clock advance, checkout wake derivation and the active resident
  location. No queue, actor, lock, task, timer, cache, retry or fallback state
  is added;
- reject alternatives that move the FIFO prefix into publisher-local storage,
  batch several resident envelopes into a second owner, commit progress after
  every endpoint, or keep an atomic in-flight bit outside the authority. They
  respectively duplicate ownership, enlarge cancellation rollback, add writes
  or create another publication authority.

